---
import ProjectPageLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectPageLayout.astro';
import SpecTable from '../../components/SpecTable.astro';
import MetricCallout from '../../components/MetricCallout.astro';

const specRows = [
  { label: 'Language',              value: 'Python 3'          },
  { label: 'Thermo. Property Lib.', value: 'CoolProp'          },
  { label: 'N₂O Error (Initial)',   value: '35%'               },
  { label: 'N₂O Error (After)',     value: '10.3%'             },
  { label: 'Orifice Diameter',      value: "0.0468''"          },
  { label: 'Area per Orifice',      value: "0.00172'' ²"       },
  { label: 'Sized Injector To',     value: '69 Orifices'       },
  { label: 'Discharge Coefficient', value: '0.4522'            },
];
---

<ProjectPageLayout
  title="Two-Phase N₂O Flow Model"
  description="Python two-phase model reducing N₂O mass-flow error from 35% to 10.3%."
  ghostText="NITROUS OXIDE"
  category="UCLA Rocket Project"
  tagline="To better predict oxidizer mass flow rate"
  showOverview={false}
  nextHref="/projects/vertical-test-stand"
>
  <!-- Overview + specs alongside rotating injector model (portrait video) -->
  <section class="spec-block" style="grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.2fr; align-items: start; gap: 1.5rem;">
    <div class="spec-col">
      <h2 class="section-overview">Overview</h2>
      <SpecTable rows={specRows} title="Specifications" />
    </div>
    <div class="model-wrap">
      <video
        class="model-video"
        src="/assets/projects/flow_model/injector_model.mp4"
        autoplay loop muted playsinline
        aria-label="Rotating SolidWorks model of the hybrid injector"
      ></video>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="metrics-row" aria-label="Key metrics">
    <MetricCallout value="69"    unit="orifices" description="Injector orifices"          />
    <MetricCallout value="10.3"  unit="%"         description="Final N₂O mass-flow error" />
    <MetricCallout value="2.654" unit="lbm/s"     description="Predicted ox flow rate"    />
  </section>

  <section class="the-work">

    <!-- ── Problem ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Problem</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          Hybrid rocket injectors using saturated-liquid nitrous oxide have a
          modeling problem: the Single-Phase Incompressible (SPI) model
        </p>
        <div class="equation" aria-label="m-dot equals C sub d times A sub c times the square root of 2 rho 1 times the quantity P 1 minus P 2">
          <span class="eq-body">ṁ = C<sub>d</sub> · A<sub>c</sub> · √<span class="eq-rad">2ρ<sub>1</sub>(P<sub>1</sub> − P<sub>2</sub>)</span></span>
          <span class="eq-tag" aria-hidden="true">SPI</span>
        </div>
        <p class="work-text">
          assumes non-flashing liquid and overpredicts mass flow by up to 30%
          when nitrous accelerates through the atomizing injector and drops
          below the saturation pressure, starting vapor formation. The
          Homogeneous Equilibrium Model (HEM)
        </p>
        <div class="equation" aria-label="m-dot equals C sub d times A sub c times rho 2 times the square root of 2 times the quantity h 1 minus h 2">
          <span class="eq-body">ṁ = C<sub>d</sub> · A<sub>c</sub> · ρ<sub>2</sub> · √<span class="eq-rad">2(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)</span></span>
          <span class="eq-tag" aria-hidden="true">HEM</span>
        </div>
        <p class="work-text">
          corrects for phase change by assuming full thermodynamic equilibrium
          between phases, but underpredicts flow, and neither model is perfect.
          Injector sizing in our program paired waterflow testing with SPI
          alone, which is a terrible forward predictor. Two-phase nitrous oxide
          flow through short orifices remains an active research area with no
          consensus. The need was a validated forward predictor.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Approach ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Approach</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          I implemented the Non-Homogeneous Non-Equilibrium (NHNE) model, a
          weighted combination of SPI and HEM controlled by a single factor
          <var>κ</var>. The methodology we used was a waterflow-test-derived
          discharge coefficient (C<sub>d</sub>), which captures our
          geometry-specific losses, and used saturation property lookups in
          Python for nitrous oxide to produce a forward mass-flow prediction
          across the operating pressure-drop range. Calibrating <var>κ</var>
          against oxidizer flow measured in a previous static fire made the
          abstract physics match our actual injector geometry. Choke detection,
          specifically the pressure ratio P<sub>2</sub>/P<sub>sat</sub> at
          which further increasing upstream pressure no longer increases mass
          flow, is computed directly from a thermodynamic lookup, creating an
          upper bound for any sizing decision.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Build ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Build</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          The implementation uses Python 3 with CoolProp for fluid properties
          through property lookups (inputs: pressure, temperature — outputs:
          density, enthalpy, vapor quality). NumPy was used for vectorized flow
          math, Matplotlib for plotting, and pandas for terminal output. The
          project is in a GitHub repository with three scripts:
          <code>inputs.py</code> holds all parameters, <code>model.py</code> is
          the pure forward model from literature, and
          <code>prediction.py</code> sweeps orifice counts to find the quantity
          that best meets the target oxidizer flow rate.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Analysis ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Analysis</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          Calibration swept <var>κ</var> across 50 values from 0 to 3.0,
          evaluating predicted mass flow against the 1.85 lb/s measured from
          the oxidizer tank load cell slope during the 14-second liquid phase
          of the previous static fire. The key finding was that pure-HEM
          over-predicted measured flow by 10%, and blending in any SPI only
          worsened accuracy. Choke detection returned a critical pressure ratio
          P<sub>2</sub>/P<sub>sat</sub> of 0.765, closely matching the
          empirically established value of approximately 0.8 derived from over
          500 cold-flow tests in published literature, independently confirming
          the results. The 10% over-prediction falls within the ±15% accuracy
          band reported at appropriate discharge coefficients
          (C<sub>d</sub>), so the validation passed both average mass flow
          within published accuracy and choke pressure ratio matching empirical
          expectations. Honestly, the 10% gap is likely water-flow
          C<sub>d</sub> being slightly higher than effective two-phase
          C<sub>d</sub>, and feed-line two-phase effects at the manifold.
        </p>
        <!-- Calibration curves — ~16:10 landscape chart -->
        <figure class="proj-figure">
          <img src="/assets/projects/flow_model/calibration_curves_at_best_kappa.png"
               alt="SPI vs HEM calibration curves at best kappa — mass-flow comparison" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Shows the SPI model substantially overpredicts mass flow relative to the clamped HEM behavior, with the measured flow rate landing below the HEM critical limit</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <!-- Iteration results — ~16:9 landscape chart -->
        <figure class="proj-figure">
          <img src="/assets/projects/flow_model/iteration_results.png"
               alt="Orifice count sweep optimization results" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>The optimization works by sweeping the number of orifices, comparing the predicted mass flow against the target, and selecting the recommended count of 69 where the design-point curve intersects the target</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Result ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Result</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          My calibrated model sized our flight injector to 69 orifices, with
          the orifice-count sweep selecting it. On our program&rsquo;s second
          hybrid engine static fire, the final fire before flight, the model
          predicted mass flow (2.693 lbm/s) with a −6.71% error relative to
          measured (2.88 lbm/s), well within the ±15% accuracy band reported in
          literature. The legacy SPI approach lacks any physical reality and
          cannot represent phase change, so it systematically overpredicts flow
          in the flashing regime. This establishes a calibrated two-phase flow
          model for the program&rsquo;s injector development, replacing a
          waterflow-only sizing method with a quantitative, reproducible
          process that can be recalibrated. I made sure to document everything
          for future students.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Reflection ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Reflection</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          None of this would have been possible without Brian J. Solomon from
          Utah State University and his 2011 M.S. report,
          <a href="/assets/projects/flow_model/BrianJSolomon.pdf"
             target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;Engineering Model to
          Calculate Mass Flow Rate of a Two-Phase Saturated Fluid Through an
          Injector Orifice.&rdquo;</a> The residual ~10% error is consistent
          with HEM&rsquo;s limitations: it assumes equal velocity for liquid
          and vapor phases. Closing that gap would require instrumentation that
          our program does not have. Another limiting factor is that we
          continue to use C<sub>d</sub> derived from water flows.
        </p>
        <figure class="proj-figure">
          <video class="media-landscape" autoplay loop muted playsinline
                 aria-label="Injector water-flow test">
            <source src="/assets/projects/flow_model/waterflow.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <figcaption>Injector water flow — produces the discharge coefficient (C<sub>d</sub>) used by the model</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

  </section>
</ProjectPageLayout>

<style>
  /* Portrait model wrap — injector_model.mp4 is 9:16, shown at 3:4 crop */
  .model-wrap {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 3 / 4;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--bg);
  }
  .model-video {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }
  .spec-col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  .spec-col :global(.section-overview) {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
    font-weight: var(--fw-black);
    color: var(--text-100);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* ── Display equations — instrumentation-style readout ─────── */
  .equation {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 1.1rem 4.5rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--grid-line);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-90);
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    max-width: 72ch;
  }
  .equation sub {
    font-size: 0.68em;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
  /* Radical bar — overline spanning the radicand */
  .eq-rad {
    border-top: 1px solid currentColor;
    padding: 0.1em 0.25em 0 0.1em;
  }
  .eq-tag {
    position: absolute;
    right: 1rem;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--text-35);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .equation {
      padding: 1rem 1rem 2.2rem;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
    }
    .eq-tag {
      top: auto;
      bottom: 0.6rem;
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  /* Inline math variables */
  .work-text var {
    font-style: italic;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
  .work-text code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--text-90);
  }
  .work-text a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }
  .work-text a:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* Water-flow video — 16:9 landscape */
  .media-landscape {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
    background: var(--surface);
  }
</style>
